---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Ticker from '../components/Ticker.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const learn = [
  {
    title: 'Critical thinking',
    body: 'How to ask a better question, test an idea, and change your mind when the evidence says to. We practice it on their own project every week, never on a worksheet.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Skills adults actually use',
    body: 'Talking to a stranger about your idea. Writing a letter that gets a reply. Pricing something. Setting up a store. The things grown-ups do at work, sized for a kid who means it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Grit',
    body: 'Prototypes break and plans change. Learning to keep going when the first version is bad is most of the work, and it can be taught.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Finishing',
    body: 'Every learner leaves with something that exists: a product, a prototype, a plan, a business with a name.',
  },
  {
    title: 'The tools professionals use',
    body: 'SketchUp, AutoCAD, Unreal Engine, Python. The versions people use at work, instead of Scratch Jr or a watered down stand-in. By third grade, kids in my programs can reload a 3D printer and sketch out a wireframe with a UI and pseudo code. The goal is to get them ready for technology that does not exist yet.',
    wide: true,
  },
];

const steps = [
  {
    label: 'The journal',
    body: 'Every learner starts with my critical thinking journal. They keep it on their own, at least one entry a week, and it is where the idea gets sharp before anyone touches a tool.',
  },
  {
    label: 'The cohort',
    body: 'Guided sessions twice a week, where I teach the next skill their project needs, plus open makerspace hours to build, use the gear, and be around other kids who are also making something.',
  },
  {
    label: 'The showcase',
    body: 'At the end, they put the finished thing in front of people who take it seriously. The strongest work earns a company visit, a workshop, or for the older band, an internship conversation.',
  },
];
---
<Base current="home">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">San Francisco. Ages 6 to 14. In person.</p>
        <h1>January is full.<br />Get on <em>the list.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          AB Combinator is a cohort for kids who have an idea and want it to exist. A game, a business, an invention, a letter to the governor. I teach the thinking and the skills it takes to make it. They make it.
        </p>
        <div class="row">
          <button type="button" class="btn primary" data-open-waitlist>Join the waitlist</button>
          <a href={base + "/about"} class="btn">What is this?</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="win status">
        <div class="win-bar"><span>Cohorts</span></div>
        <div class="win-body">
          <div class="status-row"><span>Alpha cohort</span><strong>In session now</strong></div>
          <div class="status-row"><span>January 2027</span><strong class="is-full">Full</strong></div>
          <div class="status-row"><span>The one after that</span><strong class="is-open">Waitlist open</strong></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Ticker />

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">Why this exists</p>
          <h2>Kids have real ideas. Almost nobody treats them that way.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="lede">
            For years, parents asked me the same question. My kid wants to build this thing. Where do I send them?
          </p>
          <p>
            The honest answer was usually nowhere. There are coding camps. There are pitch competitions. There is almost nothing that takes a nine year old's idea seriously, puts real tools and real adults in front of it, and teaches them how to think it through when it gets hard.
          </p>
          <p>
            So I built it. The kid drives. I point at resources, teach the skill they need next, and get out of the way.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="list-head">What they learn</h3>
      <div class="deflist">
        {learn.map((item) => (
          <div class={item.wide ? 'wide' : undefined}>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p class="soft">{item.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>How a cohort works</h2>
      <div class="steps">
        {steps.map((s) => (
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-label">{s.label}</div>
            <p>{s.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="bands-note">
        Cohorts are split into narrow age bands, 6 to 8, 9 to 11, and 12 to 14, on separate days, and kept small on purpose. The kid applies, not the parent. Spots are limited and I do turn families down, because this only works if every kid in the room wants to be there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Who's running this</p>
        <h2>Hi, I'm Amanda Grutza.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <p class="lede">I am a tech nerd and business human who is supremely passionate about helping other people build and create things that they previously thought were impossible. Personally, I find that nothing is impossible as long as you have the patience to sit down and figure it out.</p>
        <p>
          I have spent over a decade teaching design, fabrication, and whatever technology was newest at the time, including building the design and technology program at Portfolio School in New York and leading tech and innovation at a school in San Francisco. The curriculum this cohort runs on is mine. I built it independently and I have been sharpening it the whole time.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the business side, I was director of ecommerce and the technology stack at a nine-figure CPG company. Today I run a technology and digital consulting company that works with B2B and B2C brands with eight figures in Series A and B funding, including native AI companies, and I'm a HubSpot technology partner with a set of apps that keep CRM data safe, clean, and recoverable when something goes wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
          Almost nobody has done both, and that is why I can teach this. I know what it takes to turn an idea into a working thing because I have done it where the stakes were real, and I know how to teach it because I have spent years doing that too. AB Combinator is me finally answering the question parents kept asking. I'm building it in public, one cohort at a time.
        </p>
        <p><a href={base + "/about"}>More about the program</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <div class="wrap">
      <hr class="rule" />
      <div class="cta-inner">
        <h2>Cohorts are limited, spots are based on application content.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          January is full. The waitlist is for what comes next. Parents and kids each get their own form, because the kid should want this too.
        </p>
        <button type="button" class="btn primary" data-open-waitlist>Join the waitlist</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { padding-top: clamp(48px, 8vw, 96px); padding-bottom: clamp(48px, 7vw, 88px); }
  .hero-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 40px;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .hero-grid { grid-template-columns: 1.3fr 1fr; align-items: center; gap: 64px; }
  }
  .hero h1 { margin-bottom: 22px; }
  .hero .lede { margin-bottom: 24px; }

  .status .win-body { padding: 8px 20px; }
  .status-row {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .status-row:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
  .is-full { background: var(--pink); color: var(--ink); padding: 1px 6px; }
  .is-open { background: var(--yellow); color: var(--ink); padding: 1px 6px; }

  .list-head {
    margin-top: clamp(40px, 6vw, 64px);
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    color: var(--ultra);
  }
  .deflist .wide { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  .deflist .wide p { max-width: 70ch; }

  .bands-note {
    margin-top: 28px;
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
  }

  .cta { padding-top: 0; }
  .cta-inner {
    padding-top: clamp(48px, 7vw, 80px);
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    justify-items: start;
    max-width: 640px;
  }
</style>
